-- | A refusal that survives, for any Canton application.
--
-- THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS FOR. In Daml a failed `assertMsg` aborts the whole
-- transaction. Nothing is written. After a refused action the ledger looks
-- exactly as it would if the application had never tried, so the only record
-- is whatever the operator's own process wrote afterwards, about itself.
--
-- That asymmetry runs the wrong way. What succeeded is corroborated by the
-- ledger. What was stopped is the operator's word, and it is the half a
-- regulator asks about. EU AI Act Article 12 and DORA both ask an institution
-- to evidence that a control OPERATED, and a control that operates produces
-- refusals.
--
-- Note this is specific to ledgers that do not commit failed transactions.
-- On Ethereum a reverted transaction is still included in a block and still
-- costs gas, so the attempt is visible. In Daml the transaction never commits.
-- The pattern below is therefore worth more here than on a public chain.
--
-- THE PATTERN. Stop aborting. Evaluate the rule and commit either way: if it
-- passes, do the thing; if it fails, create one of these and do nothing else.
-- The contract id and the ledger time then come from the ledger rather than
-- from the operator, and cannot be invented, backdated or reordered.
--
--     now <- getTime
--     case refusalReason ... of
--       Some rule -> do
--         _ <- create RefusalRecord with
--           owner; actor; action = "withdraw"; detail = show amount
--           rule; refusedAt = now; auditor
--         pure ()
--       None -> do
--         -- the real work
--
-- The pattern was named `RejectedAttempt` by Federico_Rodriguez, a Canton
-- Community Tech Partner, on forum.canton.network/t/9114 in September 2026.
-- He also named the limit it does not fix, which is written below.
module Refusal where

-- | One attempt that a rule stopped.
--
-- Deliberately says nothing about money. `action` and `detail` are the
-- application's own words for what was tried, so a payment app, a lending
-- protocol and a collateral engine can all use this without one of them
-- carrying a field that means nothing to it.
template RefusalRecord
  with
    owner : Party
      -- ^ Who is accountable for the system that refused. A signatory,
      -- because a refusal nobody signs is a log line.
    actor : Party
      -- ^ Who tried. Also a signatory: both sides of the attempt are on the
      -- record, so neither can later say the other invented it.
    action : Text
      -- ^ What was attempted, in the application's own vocabulary.
      -- "withdraw", "substitute collateral", "settle".
    detail : Text
      -- ^ The particulars, encoded however the application likes. Keep it
      -- short and keep it true: this is the field a human reads first.
    rule : Text
      -- ^ WHICH rule refused it, word for word as the application states it
      -- elsewhere. "refused" on its own is not evidence of a control; the
      -- name of the control that fired is.
    refusedAt : Time
      -- ^ From getTime, which is ledger effective time rather than a clock
      -- the submitter writes.
    auditor : Optional Party
      -- ^ Named once, in advance, by the owner. `None` discloses to nobody
      -- beyond the two signatories, and that is the default.
  where
    signatory owner, actor

    -- The auditor observes, and this is the part a transparent chain cannot
    -- do: the refusal is ON a ledger and is NOT public, and the one party
    -- entitled to read it was named before any of these attempts happened.
    -- They never have to ask the operator for it, which is the whole
    -- difference between evidence and a report.
    observer optional [] (\a -> [a]) auditor

    ensure action /= "" && rule /= ""

    -- | Let the auditor confirm they can read it, so "we disclosed" is
    -- checkable rather than asserted. Non-consuming: asking must not destroy
    -- the thing being asked about.
    nonconsuming choice Acknowledge : Text
      controller optional owner identity auditor
      do pure rule

-- WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, written here rather than left to be discovered.
--
-- An attempt that was never submitted leaves nothing behind. This makes a
-- refusal that HAPPENED impossible to invent, delete, backdate or reorder. It
-- does not make one that never reached the ledger appear, and no ledger
-- artefact can.
--
-- A record is archivable by its signatories. The honest claim is that removing
-- one is itself a ledger event, not that it cannot be removed.
--
-- It proves a rule fired, not that a rule is correct. Whether the fence would
-- have stopped the action had the record not been written is a fact about
-- code, not about records.
